{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Qj4LCesDgB4y3bHQp9UhHn","type":"tool_result","content":"55-\t\tt.fail(n, \"rev_parse\", err)\n56-\t\treturn false\n57-\t}\n58-\tif head != pr.HeadSHA {\n59-\t\tt.log.Info(\"merge.head_moved\", \"pr\", n, \"api_head\", pr.HeadSHA, \"branch_head\", head)\n60-\t\treturn false\n61-\t}\n62-\tgraceOver := t.sawHead(n, head)\n63-\tbases, err := d.Git.MergeBaseAll(ctx, d.RepoDir, t.mainTip, head)\n64-\tif err != nil {\n65-\t\tt.fail(n, \"merge_base\", err)\n66-\t\treturn false\n67-\t}\n68-\tif len(bases) != 1 {\n69-\t\t// A criss-cross history has several bases and the choice changes what\n70-\t\t// counts as the PR's own change: a human decides.\n71-\t\tt.log.Warn(\"merge.ambiguous_base\", \"pr\", n, \"bases\", len(bases))\n72-\t\treturn false\n73-\t}\n74-\tbase := bases[0]\n75-\n76-\t// e. Every requested review passed (by its close-out record, not the label)\n77-\t// at a head that still covers this one.\n78-\tfor _, role := range v.Roles {\n79-\t\treviewed, passed, err := closeout.ReviewPassed(ctx, d.Store, issue, n, role)\n80-\t\tif err != nil {\n81-\t\t\tt.fail(n, \"review_record\", err)\n82-\t\t\treturn false\n83-\t\t}\n84-\t\tif !passed {\n85:\t\t\tt.log.Info(\"merge.reviews_stale\", \"pr\", n, \"issue\", issue, \"role\", role, \"reason\", \"no passing close-out record\",\n86-\t\t\t\t\"hint\", \"re-request the review; fix rounds (#41) will do this\")\n87-\t\t\treturn false\n88-\t\t}\n89-\t\tok, broken, err := prgate.Covers(ctx, d.Git, d.RepoDir, reviewed, head, base, issue)\n90-\t\tif err != nil {\n91-\t\t\tt.fail(n, \"review_cover\", err)\n92-\t\t\treturn false\n93-\t\t}\n94-\t\tif !ok {\n95:\t\t\tt.log.Info(\"merge.reviews_stale\", \"pr\", n, \"issue\", issue, \"role\", role, \"reviewed\", reviewed, \"head\", head, \"paths\", len(broken),\n96-\t\t\t\t\"hint\", \"re-request the review; fix rounds (#41) will do this\")\n97-\t\t\treturn false\n98-\t\t}\n99-\t}\n100-\n101-\t// f. Closing reference and trailer.\n102-\tif !closingRef(pr.Body, issue) {\n103-\t\treturn t.needsHuman(ctx, pr, \"no_closing_ref\", \"\")\n104-\t}\n105-\tjobIDs, err := t.workerJobs(ctx, issue, pr.HeadRef)\n106-\tif err != nil {\n107-\t\tt.fail(n, \"worker_jobs\", err)\n13:// Event kinds.\n15:\tEventClaim    = \"claim\"\n16:\tEventCloseout = \"closeout\"\n17:\tEventFailure  = \"failure\"\n18:\tEventRetry    = \"retry\"\n19:\t// EventUsageLimit: a job stopped at a usage limit. It is not a failure.\n20:\tEventUsageLimit = \"usage_limit\"\n21:\t// EventKill: the Reaper is about to kill a running job; data has the reason.\n22:\tEventKill      = \"kill\"\n23:\tEventTickError = \"tick_error\"\n24:\t// EventHostUntrusted: a host-mode job of a role that reads untrusted\n26:\tEventHostUntrusted = \"host_untrusted\"\n27:\t// EventGate: the triage fan-in gate acted; data has verdict, reason and worker.\n28:\tEventGate = \"gate\"\n29:\t// EventMerge: the merger squash-merged a PR; data has pr, head and merge_sha.\n30:\tEventMerge = \"merge\"\n31:\t// EventReviewRound: a review fix round changed phase; data has round, phase, reason, pr, head, worker and roles.\n32:\tEventReviewRound = \"review_round\"\n35:// Event is one row of the append-only event log. Data holds structured fields\n37:type Event struct {\n48:// EventFilter selects events; zero values are unbounded.\n49:type EventFilter struct {\n61:func insertEvent(ctx context.Context, x execer, e Event) error {\n90:// AppendEvent appends e; a zero At means now.\n91:func (s *Store) AppendEvent(ctx context.Context, e Event) error {\n98:\treturn insertEvent(ctx, s.db, e)\n101:// Events returns matching events in ascending id order.\n102:func (s *Store) Events(ctx context.Context, f EventFilter) ([]Event, error) {\n139:\tvar out []Event\n141:\t\tvar e Event\n351-\t} else {\n352-\t\tt.log.Info(\"merge.merged\", \"pr\", n, \"issue\", issue, \"head\", head, \"merge_sha\", res.SHA)\n353-\t\tdata, _ := json.Marshal(map[string]any{\"pr\": n, \"head\": head, \"merge_sha\": res.SHA})\n354:\t\tif err := d.Store.AppendEvent(ctx, state.Event{Kind: state.EventMerge, Issue: issue, Data: data}); err != nil {\n355:\t\t\tt.log.Warn(\"merge.event_failed\", \"pr\", n, \"error\", err)\n356-\t\t}\n357-\t}\n358-\tif err := d.Effects.DeleteBranch(ctx, pr.HeadRef); err != nil {\n359-\t\tt.log.Warn(\"merge.delete_branch_failed\", \"pr\", n, \"branch\", pr.HeadRef, \"error\", err)\n360-\t}\n361-\treturn !t.dry\n362-}\n363-\n364-// resolveApps fills AppID on the statuses a pinned required rule could use: a\n365-// status posted by \"<slug>[bot]\" gets the App's id (cached per process). A\ninternal/merger/loop_test.go:120:\t\t{name: \"stale review (code pushed after it)\", post: func(h *harness, p pr) {\ninternal/merger/loop_test.go:410:\tif h.merged(p.n) || !strings.Contains(out, \"merge.reviews_stale\") {\ndocs/proposal.md:85:Four more labels sit outside the grammar: `ard:approved` (human go-ahead before a worker starts; counts only when a triage-or-higher collaborator added it after triage finished), `ard:tests:failed` (worker could not get tests green; set by worker close-out on the issue and the PR, cleared by a green run), `ard:keep` (hold failed-job resources past 24 h) and `ard:pause` (on the pinned status issue, stops all new claims). `ard labels sync` creates and updates the full set from the resolved config, idempotently, and never deletes a label: stale `ard:` labels are only reported. In `label` mode a custom `intake.label` is part of the catalogue: sync creates it with a description saying it admits the issue to triage, and switching back to the default leaves the old custom label reported as stale, never deleted. `ard labels sync` and `ard config check` reject an intake label that is one of ard's own label names, naming `intake.label`; `ard serve` logs `config.intake_invalid` once per distinct value. `--repo owner/name` names the repository; when omitted, it is read from the `origin` remote. Role names are at most 31 characters and scanner names at most 38, because GitHub caps a label at 50 characters. Worker role names may not contain an all-digit segment (`eng-12`), so branch names stay unambiguous.\ndocs/proposal.md:183:1. **Reconcile.** Fetch the scanner's open issues, plus a lookup of each issue that has a tracked finding and is no longer open, and update its state. Every marker line on an open issue becomes a row on that issue (a fingerprint on two open issues goes to the lowest number), so a crash between creating an issue and recording it heals here instead of filing a duplicate. Closed as completed: mark the fingerprints fixed, keeping a tombstone so a return can link the old issue. Closed as not planned or as a duplicate: suppress them for `stale.suppress_for` (default 90 days) from the close time. Deleted or transferred: drop. An issue that is reopened is tracked again and its suppression lifts. Reconcile also runs on a run that is then dropped, so state stays current.\ndocs/proposal.md:184:2. **Backpressure, in code, before the scanner starts.** The controller counts open issues with `ard:scanner:<name>`; at or above `max_open` it skips the run and logs it, autoclose scanners included (`none` and `needs-human` issues count too, and are never stale-closed, so a non-autoclose scanner with those routes stops once its issues fill `max_open` until a human closes or promotes some): a scanner at its cap does not run with a zero filing budget, so it cannot autoclose its own fixed issues until a human or the stale closer frees a slot. A global cap across all scanners (`global_max_open`) stops a bad day from burying the queue. A skipped run is not queued: it counts as a run, and the next scheduled run checks again.\ndocs/proposal.md:194:`ard rebuild-state <scanner>` takes the leader lock (it refuses to run while `ard serve` holds it), reads every issue with `ard:scanner:<name>`, open and closed, and replaces the scanner's findings and suppressions in one transaction, keeping the run history. For each fingerprint, an open issue wins (the lowest number), else the highest-numbered closed issue decides: completed leaves a `fixed` tombstone, not planned or duplicate leaves none. Independently, the highest-numbered not-planned or duplicate close gives a suppression until `closed_at + stale.suppress_for`, kept even when expired. It prints what it adds, removes and changes; with `--dry-run` it prints and writes nothing. The dry-run state database holds synthetic issues that are not on GitHub, so it is never rebuilt. Only scanners with a `scan` executable can be rebuilt. One known gap: reopening an old issue that shares a fingerprint with a newer one can leave the database different from a rebuild until the next rebuild-state.\ndocs/proposal.md:202:**The stale closer is a built-in scanner.** It has no role directory and needs no job slot, executor or worktree; its settings live in the top-level `stale:` block of `config.yaml`. It is a plain controller loop that runs every 24 hours and is skipped while paused. It closes scanner issues still at `ard:pending` with no activity (issues filed under `route: none` or `needs-human` lack `ard:pending` and are never candidates; #204 may widen this) for `stale.inactive_for` (default 30 days), as not planned, with a comment. Activity means a timeline event by anyone other than the ard App. It never closes an issue that has a `:running` label, `ard:hold`, `ard:needs-human`, a linked open PR, or a human author (any author other than the App), and it closes nothing when the client is not authenticated as an App. It only comments and closes: suppression happens on the owning scanner's next reconcile, which suppresses every not-planned close for `stale.suppress_for`. A reopened issue gets a fresh comment when it goes stale again.\ndocs/proposal.md:214:| stale | built-in | 24 h | none | none |\ndocs/proposal.md:261:**Sidecar repos are context, read-only.** A role's sidecar (for example `vpe-specs`) is cloned by the executor, not the loop: the loop passes the role's resolved sidecar (repo and ref) on the job. The executor keeps one shallow (depth 1) bare object cache per sidecar repo at `~/.ard/sidecars/<controlled-owner>/<controlled-repo>/<sidecar-owner>/<sidecar-repo>.git`, shared by every job of that controlled repo, and exports the commit into the job home as `<job home>/sidecar` (plain files: no `.git`, `chmod -R a-w` in both modes, and a `:ro` mount under Docker). The job sees it at `$ARD_SIDECAR`. `ref` is a branch, tag or full 40-character SHA (an abbreviated SHA is a config error). A SHA already in the cache needs no network; a branch or tag is re-fetched (depth 1) for every job. Whatever the ref, `Prepare` resolves it to a full commit SHA and records it on the job's handle (and in the `executor.sidecar_ready` log), so a job is reproducible. Symlinks and submodules in the sidecar are rejected and the tree is capped at 64 MiB, like config snapshots. A fetch failure (network, auth, a stale lock, which the executor clears by rebuilding the cache once) is the transient `clone_failed`; a ref or SHA that does not exist, or a repo the App cannot read, is a permanent failure that names the sidecar. A role that wants a spec changed says so in `plan.md`; editing the sidecar is out of scope for v1.\ndocs/proposal.md:267:Local-executor details: the role directory (and its `Dockerfile`) is mounted from the config snapshot at the job's SHA, not from the working tree. The job worktree is always detached at a SHA resolved from the job's ref (the executor never checks out a branch; the committer owns branch worktrees). The controller's clone is a bare repository ard owns at `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` (lowercased), with no remote, no stored credential and auto-gc off (`gc.auto=0`, `maintenance.auto=false`). `ard serve` creates it on first start and seeds it with one fetch of `main` (up to 30 min; if that fails, serve starts paused and the next start retries), and later starts reuse it. Config loads and committer lanes fetch into it, and every committer and executor worktree is cut from it (lanes under `~/.ard/work/<owner>/<repo>/lanes/`). `ard serve` never writes to the operator's checkout: it reads it only for the `origin` fallback of `--repo` and the local `paused` key. To rebuild a damaged clone, stop serve, `rm -rf` it and restart. Upgrading from a version that used the checkout: ard deletes its old lane and job worktree directories without running git there, so the old `refs/ard/*` refs and the dangling worktree registrations stay in the checkout until you remove them (`git for-each-ref --format='delete %(refname)' refs/ard/ | git update-ref --stdin; git worktree prune`). Every `git worktree add`, `remove` and `prune` on the controller's clone, by the executor and the committer's lanes alike, takes one in-process lock keyed by the clone's common git directory, because git's worktree bookkeeping is not safe under concurrent admin commands; fetches, commits and pushes do not take it. Each such command also has a 30 s deadline, and cleanup runs even when the job's context is cancelled, so one hung git cannot stall every lane. Do not run `git worktree prune` (or add/remove) in `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` by hand while `ard serve` runs: the lock is per process and cannot cover a human's git. The operator's own checkout is free to use. The host keeps two files per job: `<base>.log` (stdout and stderr combined, the heartbeat source and the committed log) and `<base>.stdout` (stdout only, parsed by the adapter). A read-only job (`ReadOnly`, used by scanners) gets the worktree mounted `:ro` under Docker, or `chmod -R a-w` on the host. The heartbeat is the log file's mtime while the PID is alive; liveness alone is not a beat. Phase 0 local jobs do not survive a controller restart: the pipes belong to the controller, so after a restart the job reports `orphaned` (PID alive, start time verified) or `gone`, and the Reaper kills and fails it. The executor is not an effect: it never calls GitHub or commits, so jobs run normally under `--dry-run`. Sidecar auth: the sidecar fetch runs in the controller with the GitHub App installation token in a host-scoped `http.<url>.extraheader` passed through git's environment, never argv; the job receives plain files and no credential, and the cache stores no remote and no token. The App must be installed on the sidecar repo with contents:read. Host-mode jobs can reach `~/.ard/sidecars`, but it holds no credential. Host-mode jobs run as the controller's uid: they can read the GitHub App key and `~/.config/gh` by absolute path, write the controller's clone, and undo `chmod a-w`. Host mode is allowed for every role ([#84](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/84)), but a role that reads untrusted input (every triage, worker and reviewer role, and an LLM scanner) warns loudly when it has no `Dockerfile`; deterministic scanners are exempt, and nothing warns under `executor: k8s`, where every job runs in a container. `ard config check` and the first successful config load of `ard serve` (and any role a later config adds) log one `executor.host_untrusted` WARN per such role and also write it to stderr, so it survives `--log-level`. The local executor logs the WARN again for every host-mode job of such a role and appends a `host_untrusted` row to the `events` table. Each warning lists what the job can reach, including `~/.ard` (state, config snapshots, the pause file, other jobs' worktrees and logs). The owner accepts this risk while ard runs only on owner-controlled repos, and revisits it when moving to the spark cluster, where every job runs in a container once the k8s executor (#54) lands. Until then the Gate 1 dry-run soak on spark (see Rollout and Delivery) runs `ard serve` with the local executor in a pod, and the deterministic cve scanner runs there as a host subprocess of the controller container: its role directory has no `Dockerfile`, and the `ard` image carries its pinned tools. Only deterministic scanners run in that pod: it starts `ard serve --loops scanners,stale,reaper` ([#187](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/187)), so the triage, worker and reviewer loops never run there, even though `.ard/config.yaml` on `main` has agent roles. `--loops` takes a comma-separated list of loop names (scanners, stale, triage, workers, reviewers, reviewfix, merger, janitor, reaper, budget), defaults to every loop, and rejects an unknown name with exit 2. `ard serve` and `ard config check` refuse to run on git older than 2.47.0, because the committer's fetches fail on older versions.\ndocs/proposal.md:288:**Usage is recorded for every job, whatever its outcome.** The Finisher stores each agent step's usage (the models it reported, the four token counts, input, output, cache write and cache read, and the cost) in local state, keyed by job and executor step id, so a replay or a Reaper re-parse never counts a step twice. A worker job's agent and fix steps are summed: the job's totals come from state, not from the last step. A failed, retried or usage-limited job keeps its cost, and the totals go into the `reaper.finish` log. Close-out commits the job's tracking file `.ard/<issue#>/costs/<job-id>.json` (stage, role, attempt, outcome, cause, models, tokens by type, cost, start and end time, PR) in the job's normal lane in the same commit as its log: `main` for triage, the PR branch for worker and reviewer jobs. It is controller-written and holds no free text. The cost is the agent CLI's list-price estimate, not a bill; a job whose adapter reported none records `null`. **The issue body carries a cost table.** Reaching `closed` marks the issue's cost table dirty in the same state transaction, and the `costs` loop (one minute, one writer per issue) writes it: it renders the table from state (one row per closed job, oldest first, \"#k\" for the k-th run of a stage and role; tokens by type, cost, models, outcome, a link to the job's comment or review, the PR; a Total row), reads the issue, splices only its marked block (`<!-- ard:cost:begin -->` to `<!-- ard:cost:end -->`, appended after the human text on first write), writes the body through the effect layer, reads it again to verify, and re-splices and writes once more if another writer replaced the block. A failed table write is logged and retried on a later tick (ten failures, then it waits for the next close-out on the issue); it never fails or delays a close-out. The table holds only counts, dollars, model ids, a fixed outcome vocabulary (`done`, `changes`, `stale`, `needs-human`, `retry`, `usage limit`, `failed: <cause kind | exit code | result>`) and links, never agent text, a reason, a prompt, a log or a host path. It is capped at 5,000 bytes (and by GitHub's 65,536-character body limit): the oldest rows fold into one \"earlier jobs (k)\" row while Total stays exact. A claim that failed before the agent ran has no close-out and no row. The scanner keeps the block across its own body rewrites (it cuts the valid block off, renders, and puts it back), and takes the same per-issue lock. **Residual race:** GitHub has no conditional body write, so there is one GET-to-PATCH round trip in which a human save from a stale editor is overwritten; ard never writes text it did not just read, re-reads to verify, retries once, and GitHub's edit history recovers the lost edit. In dry-run the write goes to the ledger only and the table is not verified or linked. The `costs` loop keeps running while the controller is paused: it is bookkeeping about work already done, so it reports `Claims() == false` like the Reaper. A review's row links to its review because `Effects.CreateReview` returns the review id (stored in the close-out record).\ndocs/proposal.md:310:4. **Run the agent** with `.ard/workers/<role>/prompt.txt` and these untrusted inputs: `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md` (written by the loop, outside `.ard/<issue#>/`, never committed), `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md` when triage wrote one and `.ard/<issue#>/issue.md` when a scanner filed the issue (each only as a regular file, never through a symlink), the triage role files and logs, and, in a fix step, `.ard-input/<issue#>/test-output.log` (the untrusted header, the test command and its exit code, and the last 64 KiB of the test log). In a review round, `.ard-input/<issue#>/review-feedback.md` (the untrusted header, the round, the committed role files of the reviewers that asked for changes and the pull request comments of people, cut at 64 KiB), and the reviewer role files and logs on the branch are untrusted inputs too. The agent edits code and writes `.ard/<issue#>/worker-<role>.md`; the executor scrubs a stale copy first.\ndocs/proposal.md:332:6. **A moved head is not a pass.** Just before close-out the loop compares the branch with the pinned commit. If it gained code outside `.ard/<issue#>/` meanwhile, a `changes` verdict is still posted and labelled (it is the conservative one), but an `approve` is posted without `:done`: the request label stays and the review runs again on the new head. The reviewed commit is recorded in the close-out record so the merger (#46) re-verifies, with `prgate.Covers` against the reviewed head recorded by the review's claim, that no code of the PR changed since before merging: a small window remains between the head check and close-out adding `:done`. A review closed out by the Reaper after a restart has no head information, so an approve is treated as stale.\ndocs/proposal.md:333:7. **A gone PR is stale, not failed.** A human may merge or close the PR, or GitHub may delete its branch, while a review runs. At close-out the controller reads the PR before committing. If it was merged or closed, or the committer reports the branch gone, the job ends `stale`: nothing is committed to the branch, only `:running` is removed, there is no `ard:executor:failed` and no failure comment, and `review.pr_gone` is logged with the state (`merged`, `closed` or `branch_gone`). The PR review is still posted, best-effort: if posting fails the error is logged and the job still closes. A failed PR read falls through to the commit, where a missing branch gives the same outcome. Gate 2's failure rate counts only `failed` jobs, so these are not executor failures. A worker has no equivalent: its PR is a draft that cannot be merged while it runs, so a branch deleted mid-job stays a real failure.\ndocs/proposal.md:358:- every requested `ard:review:<role>` is `:done` and none is `:changes` or `:running` (any `ard:review:*:changes` or `:running` label blocks, configured role or not), and each review is proven by the newest close-out record of that role on this PR (outcome done, head not moved), not by the label, which a person with triage access can add, and stands at a head that still covers the current one. The head rule is shared with the reviewer's mark-ready (`internal/prgate`): a head is covered when no path outside `.ard/<issue#>/` changed since the reviewed commit, or, for the merger, when every such path came from `main` (an update-branch merge does not invalidate a review, but a path both the PR and `main` changed since the review does). A stale review is only logged: a person must re-request it; fix rounds rerun reviewers only after a worker fix\ndocs/proposal.md:408:stale: { inactive_for: 30d, suppress_for: 90d }\ndocs/proposal.md:452:- **Kill switch.** Four sources stop new claims, scanner runs (filing and autoclose), stale closes and merges: a local pause file `~/.ard/pause/<owner>/<repo>` outside the repository (the path is lowercased; `ard serve` creates the directory, so `touch` pauses and `rm` resumes), `paused: true` in the `config.yaml` in the host's working tree, `ard:pause` on the pinned status issue, and `paused: true` in the config on `origin/main`. They are checked cheapest first, before every claiming tick. The delay is at most one loop interval plus 10% jitter; a tick already running may finish its claims. `SIGUSR1` (Unix only) makes every loop run one tick now, logged as `controller.wake`; the tick still passes the kill switch and the job pool, and wakes that arrive while a tick runs coalesce into one extra tick. The gate answers one controller-wide question and has no per-kind checks, so every loop that writes (scanner filing and autoclose, stale closes, merges, claims), except the Reaper, the cost-table writer and in-flight close-outs, must report `Claims() == true` and is skipped while paused. Running jobs, in-flight close-outs, the Reaper and the `costs` loop continue: the table is bookkeeping about work already done, not new work. The check fails closed: an unreadable status issue, a failed fetch of `origin/main`, or a config that fails to load counts as paused. The pause file pauses when anything exists at the path (a file, a directory or a symlink; content is ignored), and a path that cannot be examined, for example because `pause/<owner>` is a regular file, also counts as paused. The local working-tree `config.yaml` is read only for its `paused` key; a missing, unreadable or invalid file does not pause by itself, but a readable one that says `paused: true` does. The pause file and the local `paused` are the kill switches that nothing pushed to GitHub can lift. Config and role directories otherwise come from `origin/main` at the same SHA the tick's jobs use (see Configuration). `ard serve` remembers the status issue number in `_controller.db` (`meta` key `status_issue`). At startup it reads that issue directly and adopts it if it is still open, titled `ard status`, carries the `<!-- ard:status -->` marker and has the App as author; otherwise it searches by those same tests, and creates and pins the issue if none matches. Search is eventually consistent, so the remembered number is what stops a quick restart from creating a second issue; if two still match, the lowest number wins. A cached issue that cannot be read for a reason other than not-found falls back to search rather than aborting startup. A dry-run never stores its synthetic (negative) number; a real number found by search is stored only in the separate dry-run database. A second `ard serve` on the same repo exits with an error: the leader lock is an `flock` on `~/.ard/locks/<owner>/<repo>.lock` (local filesystem only), shared by live and dry-run.\ndocs/proposal.md:491:    P0[\"Phase 0: Skeleton<br/>controller and config<br/>label bootstrap<br/>committer, local executor<br/>cve and stale scanners<br/>dry-run mode\"] -->|\"Gate 1: 1 week dry-run, 0 duplicate issues\"| P1[\"Phase 1: First loop<br/>lead + engineer triage<br/>engineer worker<br/>eng-review<br/>Reaper<br/>human approval gate\"]\ndocs/proposal.md:502:Phase 0 runs in dry-run: the controller logs every issue, label and commit it would make, and does none of them. Dry-run keeps its own state (`~/.ard/state-dryrun/`) and records would-file findings under synthetic negative issue numbers, so repeated scans don't re-propose them and `max_open` is exercised. The ledger is `effects.jsonl` in that directory: every intended effect (issue, label, comment, commit, push, merge) is appended as one JSON line, and Gate 1 counts duplicate issues there by repeated `<!-- ard:fp=... -->` markers in `CreateIssue` bodies. Gate 1 needs only the deterministic path (CLI, config, GitHub client, effects, state, labels, executor, committer, controller, pool and the cve and stale scanners); the agent adapter, close-out and redaction are built during the soak week.\ninternal/merger/doc.go:25://     does not invalidate a review (prgate.Covers with the merge base). A stale","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bd6c6d47-ab1b-465f-87a4-b9431b243069","uuid":"484cc42a-dba7-4fb7-a6e7-1b4831212165","timestamp":"2026-11-23T19:19:32.399Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"55-\t\tt.fail(n, \"rev_parse\", err)\n56-\t\treturn false\n57-\t}\n58-\tif head != pr.HeadSHA {\n59-\t\tt.log.Info(\"merge.head_moved\", \"pr\", n, \"api_head\", pr.HeadSHA, \"branch_head\", head)\n60-\t\treturn false\n61-\t}\n62-\tgraceOver := t.sawHead(n, head)\n63-\tbases, err := d.Git.MergeBaseAll(ctx, d.RepoDir, t.mainTip, head)\n64-\tif err != nil {\n65-\t\tt.fail(n, \"merge_base\", err)\n66-\t\treturn false\n67-\t}\n68-\tif len(bases) != 1 {\n69-\t\t// A criss-cross history has several bases and the choice changes what\n70-\t\t// counts as the PR's own change: a human decides.\n71-\t\tt.log.Warn(\"merge.ambiguous_base\", \"pr\", n, \"bases\", len(bases))\n72-\t\treturn false\n73-\t}\n74-\tbase := bases[0]\n75-\n76-\t// e. Every requested review passed (by its close-out record, not the label)\n77-\t// at a head that still covers this one.\n78-\tfor _, role := range v.Roles {\n79-\t\treviewed, passed, err := closeout.ReviewPassed(ctx, d.Store, issue, n, role)\n80-\t\tif err != nil {\n81-\t\t\tt.fail(n, \"review_record\", err)\n82-\t\t\treturn false\n83-\t\t}\n84-\t\tif !passed {\n85:\t\t\tt.log.Info(\"merge.reviews_stale\", \"pr\", n, \"issue\", issue, \"role\", role, \"reason\", \"no passing close-out record\",\n86-\t\t\t\t\"hint\", \"re-request the review; fix rounds (#41) will do this\")\n87-\t\t\treturn false\n88-\t\t}\n89-\t\tok, broken, err := prgate.Covers(ctx, d.Git, d.RepoDir, reviewed, head, base, issue)\n90-\t\tif err != nil {\n91-\t\t\tt.fail(n, \"review_cover\", err)\n92-\t\t\treturn false\n93-\t\t}\n94-\t\tif !ok {\n95:\t\t\tt.log.Info(\"merge.reviews_stale\", \"pr\", n, \"issue\", issue, \"role\", role, \"reviewed\", reviewed, \"head\", head, \"paths\", len(broken),\n96-\t\t\t\t\"hint\", \"re-request the review; fix rounds (#41) will do this\")\n97-\t\t\treturn false\n98-\t\t}\n99-\t}\n100-\n101-\t// f. Closing reference and trailer.\n102-\tif !closingRef(pr.Body, issue) {\n103-\t\treturn t.needsHuman(ctx, pr, \"no_closing_ref\", \"\")\n104-\t}\n105-\tjobIDs, err := t.workerJobs(ctx, issue, pr.HeadRef)\n106-\tif err != nil {\n107-\t\tt.fail(n, \"worker_jobs\", err)\n13:// Event kinds.\n15:\tEventClaim    = \"claim\"\n16:\tEventCloseout = \"closeout\"\n17:\tEventFailure  = \"failure\"\n18:\tEventRetry    = \"retry\"\n19:\t// EventUsageLimit: a job stopped at a usage limit. It is not a failure.\n20:\tEventUsageLimit = \"usage_limit\"\n21:\t// EventKill: the Reaper is about to kill a running job; data has the reason.\n22:\tEventKill      = \"kill\"\n23:\tEventTickError = \"tick_error\"\n24:\t// EventHostUntrusted: a host-mode job of a role that reads untrusted\n26:\tEventHostUntrusted = \"host_untrusted\"\n27:\t// EventGate: the triage fan-in gate acted; data has verdict, reason and worker.\n28:\tEventGate = \"gate\"\n29:\t// EventMerge: the merger squash-merged a PR; data has pr, head and merge_sha.\n30:\tEventMerge = \"merge\"\n31:\t// EventReviewRound: a review fix round changed phase; data has round, phase, reason, pr, head, worker and roles.\n32:\tEventReviewRound = \"review_round\"\n35:// Event is one row of the append-only event log. Data holds structured fields\n37:type Event struct {\n48:// EventFilter selects events; zero values are unbounded.\n49:type EventFilter struct {\n61:func insertEvent(ctx context.Context, x execer, e Event) error {\n90:// AppendEvent appends e; a zero At means now.\n91:func (s *Store) AppendEvent(ctx context.Context, e Event) error {\n98:\treturn insertEvent(ctx, s.db, e)\n101:// Events returns matching events in ascending id order.\n102:func (s *Store) Events(ctx context.Context, f EventFilter) ([]Event, error) {\n139:\tvar out []Event\n141:\t\tvar e Event\n351-\t} else {\n352-\t\tt.log.Info(\"merge.merged\", \"pr\", n, \"issue\", issue, \"head\", head, \"merge_sha\", res.SHA)\n353-\t\tdata, _ := json.Marshal(map[string]any{\"pr\": n, \"head\": head, \"merge_sha\": res.SHA})\n354:\t\tif err := d.Store.AppendEvent(ctx, state.Event{Kind: state.EventMerge, Issue: issue, Data: data}); err != nil {\n355:\t\t\tt.log.Warn(\"merge.event_failed\", \"pr\", n, \"error\", err)\n356-\t\t}\n357-\t}\n358-\tif err := d.Effects.DeleteBranch(ctx, pr.HeadRef); err != nil {\n359-\t\tt.log.Warn(\"merge.delete_branch_failed\", \"pr\", n, \"branch\", pr.HeadRef, \"error\", err)\n360-\t}\n361-\treturn !t.dry\n362-}\n363-\n364-// resolveApps fills AppID on the statuses a pinned required rule could use: a\n365-// status posted by \"<slug>[bot]\" gets the App's id (cached per process). A\ninternal/merger/loop_test.go:120:\t\t{name: \"stale review (code pushed after it)\", post: func(h *harness, p pr) {\ninternal/merger/loop_test.go:410:\tif h.merged(p.n) || !strings.Contains(out, \"merge.reviews_stale\") {\ndocs/proposal.md:85:Four more labels sit outside the grammar: `ard:approved` (human go-ahead before a worker starts; counts only when a triage-or-higher collaborator added it after triage finished), `ard:tests:failed` (worker could not get tests green; set by worker close-out on the issue and the PR, cleared by a green run), `ard:keep` (hold failed-job resources past 24 h) and `ard:pause` (on the pinned status issue, stops all new claims). `ard labels sync` creates and updates the full set from the resolved config, idempotently, and never deletes a label: stale `ard:` labels are only reported. In `label` mode a custom `intake.label` is part of the catalogue: sync creates it with a description saying it admits the issue to triage, and switching back to the default leaves the old custom label reported as stale, never deleted. `ard labels sync` and `ard config check` reject an intake label that is one of ard's own label names, naming `intake.label`; `ard serve` logs `config.intake_invalid` once per distinct value. `--repo owner/name` names the repository; when omitted, it is read from the `origin` remote. Role names are at most 31 characters and scanner names at most 38, because GitHub caps a label at 50 characters. Worker role names may not contain an all-digit segment (`eng-12`), so branch names stay unambiguous.\ndocs/proposal.md:183:1. **Reconcile.** Fetch the scanner's open issues, plus a lookup of each issue that has a tracked finding and is no longer open, and update its state. Every marker line on an open issue becomes a row on that issue (a fingerprint on two open issues goes to the lowest number), so a crash between creating an issue and recording it heals here instead of filing a duplicate. Closed as completed: mark the fingerprints fixed, keeping a tombstone so a return can link the old issue. Closed as not planned or as a duplicate: suppress them for `stale.suppress_for` (default 90 days) from the close time. Deleted or transferred: drop. An issue that is reopened is tracked again and its suppression lifts. Reconcile also runs on a run that is then dropped, so state stays current.\ndocs/proposal.md:184:2. **Backpressure, in code, before the scanner starts.** The controller counts open issues with `ard:scanner:<name>`; at or above `max_open` it skips the run and logs it, autoclose scanners included (`none` and `needs-human` issues count too, and are never stale-closed, so a non-autoclose scanner with those routes stops once its issues fill `max_open` until a human closes or promotes some): a scanner at its cap does not run with a zero filing budget, so it cannot autoclose its own fixed issues until a human or the stale closer frees a slot. A global cap across all scanners (`global_max_open`) stops a bad day from burying the queue. A skipped run is not queued: it counts as a run, and the next scheduled run checks again.\ndocs/proposal.md:194:`ard rebuild-state <scanner>` takes the leader lock (it refuses to run while `ard serve` holds it), reads every issue with `ard:scanner:<name>`, open and closed, and replaces the scanner's findings and suppressions in one transaction, keeping the run history. For each fingerprint, an open issue wins (the lowest number), else the highest-numbered closed issue decides: completed leaves a `fixed` tombstone, not planned or duplicate leaves none. Independently, the highest-numbered not-planned or duplicate close gives a suppression until `closed_at + stale.suppress_for`, kept even when expired. It prints what it adds, removes and changes; with `--dry-run` it prints and writes nothing. The dry-run state database holds synthetic issues that are not on GitHub, so it is never rebuilt. Only scanners with a `scan` executable can be rebuilt. One known gap: reopening an old issue that shares a fingerprint with a newer one can leave the database different from a rebuild until the next rebuild-state.\ndocs/proposal.md:202:**The stale closer is a built-in scanner.** It has no role directory and needs no job slot, executor or worktree; its settings live in the top-level `stale:` block of `config.yaml`. It is a plain controller loop that runs every 24 hours and is skipped while paused. It closes scanner issues still at `ard:pending` with no activity (issues filed under `route: none` or `needs-human` lack `ard:pending` and are never candidates; #204 may widen this) for `stale.inactive_for` (default 30 days), as not planned, with a comment. Activity means a timeline event by anyone other than the ard App. It never closes an issue that has a `:running` label, `ard:hold`, `ard:needs-human`, a linked open PR, or a human author (any author other than the App), and it closes nothing when the client is not authenticated as an App. It only comments and closes: suppression happens on the owning scanner's next reconcile, which suppresses every not-planned close for `stale.suppress_for`. A reopened issue gets a fresh comment when it goes stale again.\ndocs/proposal.md:214:| stale | built-in | 24 h | none | none |\ndocs/proposal.md:261:**Sidecar repos are context, read-only.** A role's sidecar (for example `vpe-specs`) is cloned by the executor, not the loop: the loop passes the role's resolved sidecar (repo and ref) on the job. The executor keeps one shallow (depth 1) bare object cache per sidecar repo at `~/.ard/sidecars/<controlled-owner>/<controlled-repo>/<sidecar-owner>/<sidecar-repo>.git`, shared by every job of that controlled repo, and exports the commit into the job home as `<job home>/sidecar` (plain files: no `.git`, `chmod -R a-w` in both modes, and a `:ro` mount under Docker). The job sees it at `$ARD_SIDECAR`. `ref` is a branch, tag or full 40-character SHA (an abbreviated SHA is a config error). A SHA already in the cache needs no network; a branch or tag is re-fetched (depth 1) for every job. Whatever the ref, `Prepare` resolves it to a full commit SHA and records it on the job's handle (and in the `executor.sidecar_ready` log), so a job is reproducible. Symlinks and submodules in the sidecar are rejected and the tree is capped at 64 MiB, like config snapshots. A fetch failure (network, auth, a stale lock, which the executor clears by rebuilding the cache once) is the transient `clone_failed`; a ref or SHA that does not exist, or a repo the App cannot read, is a permanent failure that names the sidecar. A role that wants a spec changed says so in `plan.md`; editing the sidecar is out of scope for v1.\ndocs/proposal.md:267:Local-executor details: the role directory (and its `Dockerfile`) is mounted from the config snapshot at the job's SHA, not from the working tree. The job worktree is always detached at a SHA resolved from the job's ref (the executor never checks out a branch; the committer owns branch worktrees). The controller's clone is a bare repository ard owns at `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` (lowercased), with no remote, no stored credential and auto-gc off (`gc.auto=0`, `maintenance.auto=false`). `ard serve` creates it on first start and seeds it with one fetch of `main` (up to 30 min; if that fails, serve starts paused and the next start retries), and later starts reuse it. Config loads and committer lanes fetch into it, and every committer and executor worktree is cut from it (lanes under `~/.ard/work/<owner>/<repo>/lanes/`). `ard serve` never writes to the operator's checkout: it reads it only for the `origin` fallback of `--repo` and the local `paused` key. To rebuild a damaged clone, stop serve, `rm -rf` it and restart. Upgrading from a version that used the checkout: ard deletes its old lane and job worktree directories without running git there, so the old `refs/ard/*` refs and the dangling worktree registrations stay in the checkout until you remove them (`git for-each-ref --format='delete %(refname)' refs/ard/ | git update-ref --stdin; git worktree prune`). Every `git worktree add`, `remove` and `prune` on the controller's clone, by the executor and the committer's lanes alike, takes one in-process lock keyed by the clone's common git directory, because git's worktree bookkeeping is not safe under concurrent admin commands; fetches, commits and pushes do not take it. Each such command also has a 30 s deadline, and cleanup runs even when the job's context is cancelled, so one hung git cannot stall every lane. Do not run `git worktree prune` (or add/remove) in `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` by hand while `ard serve` runs: the lock is per process and cannot cover a human's git. The operator's own checkout is free to use. The host keeps two files per job: `<base>.log` (stdout and stderr combined, the heartbeat source and the committed log) and `<base>.stdout` (stdout only, parsed by the adapter). A read-only job (`ReadOnly`, used by scanners) gets the worktree mounted `:ro` under Docker, or `chmod -R a-w` on the host. The heartbeat is the log file's mtime while the PID is alive; liveness alone is not a beat. Phase 0 local jobs do not survive a controller restart: the pipes belong to the controller, so after a restart the job reports `orphaned` (PID alive, start time verified) or `gone`, and the Reaper kills and fails it. The executor is not an effect: it never calls GitHub or commits, so jobs run normally under `--dry-run`. Sidecar auth: the sidecar fetch runs in the controller with the GitHub App installation token in a host-scoped `http.<url>.extraheader` passed through git's environment, never argv; the job receives plain files and no credential, and the cache stores no remote and no token. The App must be installed on the sidecar repo with contents:read. Host-mode jobs can reach `~/.ard/sidecars`, but it holds no credential. Host-mode jobs run as the controller's uid: they can read the GitHub App key and `~/.config/gh` by absolute path, write the controller's clone, and undo `chmod a-w`. Host mode is allowed for every role ([#84](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/84)), but a role that reads untrusted input (every triage, worker and reviewer role, and an LLM scanner) warns loudly when it has no `Dockerfile`; deterministic scanners are exempt, and nothing warns under `executor: k8s`, where every job runs in a container. `ard config check` and the first successful config load of `ard serve` (and any role a later config adds) log one `executor.host_untrusted` WARN per such role and also write it to stderr, so it survives `--log-level`. The local executor logs the WARN again for every host-mode job of such a role and appends a `host_untrusted` row to the `events` table. Each warning lists what the job can reach, including `~/.ard` (state, config snapshots, the pause file, other jobs' worktrees and logs). The owner accepts this risk while ard runs only on owner-controlled repos, and revisits it when moving to the spark cluster, where every job runs in a container once the k8s executor (#54) lands. Until then the Gate 1 dry-run soak on spark (see Rollout and Delivery) runs `ard serve` with the local executor in a pod, and the deterministic cve scanner runs there as a host subprocess of the controller container: its role directory has no `Dockerfile`, and the `ard` image carries its pinned tools. Only deterministic scanners run in that pod: it starts `ard serve --loops scanners,stale,reaper` ([#187](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/187)), so the triage, worker and reviewer loops never run there, even though `.ard/config.yaml` on `main` has agent roles. `--loops` takes a comma-separated list of loop names (scanners, stale, triage, workers, reviewers, reviewfix, merger, janitor, reaper, budget), defaults to every loop, and rejects an unknown name with exit 2. `ard serve` and `ard config check` refuse to run on git older than 2.47.0, because the committer's fetches fail on older versions.\ndocs/proposal.md:288:**Usage is recorded for every job, whatever its outcome.** The Finisher stores each agent step's usage (the models it reported, the four token counts, input, output, cache write and cache read, and the cost) in local state, keyed by job and executor step id, so a replay or a Reaper re-parse never counts a step twice. A worker job's agent and fix steps are summed: the job's totals come from state, not from the last step. A failed, retried or usage-limited job keeps its cost, and the totals go into the `reaper.finish` log. Close-out commits the job's tracking file `.ard/<issue#>/costs/<job-id>.json` (stage, role, attempt, outcome, cause, models, tokens by type, cost, start and end time, PR) in the job's normal lane in the same commit as its log: `main` for triage, the PR branch for worker and reviewer jobs. It is controller-written and holds no free text. The cost is the agent CLI's list-price estimate, not a bill; a job whose adapter reported none records `null`. **The issue body carries a cost table.** Reaching `closed` marks the issue's cost table dirty in the same state transaction, and the `costs` loop (one minute, one writer per issue) writes it: it renders the table from state (one row per closed job, oldest first, \"#k\" for the k-th run of a stage and role; tokens by type, cost, models, outcome, a link to the job's comment or review, the PR; a Total row), reads the issue, splices only its marked block (`<!-- ard:cost:begin -->` to `<!-- ard:cost:end -->`, appended after the human text on first write), writes the body through the effect layer, reads it again to verify, and re-splices and writes once more if another writer replaced the block. A failed table write is logged and retried on a later tick (ten failures, then it waits for the next close-out on the issue); it never fails or delays a close-out. The table holds only counts, dollars, model ids, a fixed outcome vocabulary (`done`, `changes`, `stale`, `needs-human`, `retry`, `usage limit`, `failed: <cause kind | exit code | result>`) and links, never agent text, a reason, a prompt, a log or a host path. It is capped at 5,000 bytes (and by GitHub's 65,536-character body limit): the oldest rows fold into one \"earlier jobs (k)\" row while Total stays exact. A claim that failed before the agent ran has no close-out and no row. The scanner keeps the block across its own body rewrites (it cuts the valid block off, renders, and puts it back), and takes the same per-issue lock. **Residual race:** GitHub has no conditional body write, so there is one GET-to-PATCH round trip in which a human save from a stale editor is overwritten; ard never writes text it did not just read, re-reads to verify, retries once, and GitHub's edit history recovers the lost edit. In dry-run the write goes to the ledger only and the table is not verified or linked. The `costs` loop keeps running while the controller is paused: it is bookkeeping about work already done, so it reports `Claims() == false` like the Reaper. A review's row links to its review because `Effects.CreateReview` returns the review id (stored in the close-out record).\ndocs/proposal.md:310:4. **Run the agent** with `.ard/workers/<role>/prompt.txt` and these untrusted inputs: `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md` (written by the loop, outside `.ard/<issue#>/`, never committed), `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md` when triage wrote one and `.ard/<issue#>/issue.md` when a scanner filed the issue (each only as a regular file, never through a symlink), the triage role files and logs, and, in a fix step, `.ard-input/<issue#>/test-output.log` (the untrusted header, the test command and its exit code, and the last 64 KiB of the test log). In a review round, `.ard-input/<issue#>/review-feedback.md` (the untrusted header, the round, the committed role files of the reviewers that asked for changes and the pull request comments of people, cut at 64 KiB), and the reviewer role files and logs on the branch are untrusted inputs too. The agent edits code and writes `.ard/<issue#>/worker-<role>.md`; the executor scrubs a stale copy first.\ndocs/proposal.md:332:6. **A moved head is not a pass.** Just before close-out the loop compares the branch with the pinned commit. If it gained code outside `.ard/<issue#>/` meanwhile, a `changes` verdict is still posted and labelled (it is the conservative one), but an `approve` is posted without `:done`: the request label stays and the review runs again on the new head. The reviewed commit is recorded in the close-out record so the merger (#46) re-verifies, with `prgate.Covers` against the reviewed head recorded by the review's claim, that no code of the PR changed since before merging: a small window remains between the head check and close-out adding `:done`. A review closed out by the Reaper after a restart has no head information, so an approve is treated as stale.\ndocs/proposal.md:333:7. **A gone PR is stale, not failed.** A human may merge or close the PR, or GitHub may delete its branch, while a review runs. At close-out the controller reads the PR before committing. If it was merged or closed, or the committer reports the branch gone, the job ends `stale`: nothing is committed to the branch, only `:running` is removed, there is no `ard:executor:failed` and no failure comment, and `review.pr_gone` is logged with the state (`merged`, `closed` or `branch_gone`). The PR review is still posted, best-effort: if posting fails the error is logged and the job still closes. A failed PR read falls through to the commit, where a missing branch gives the same outcome. Gate 2's failure rate counts only `failed` jobs, so these are not executor failures. A worker has no equivalent: its PR is a draft that cannot be merged while it runs, so a branch deleted mid-job stays a real failure.\ndocs/proposal.md:358:- every requested `ard:review:<role>` is `:done` and none is `:changes` or `:running` (any `ard:review:*:changes` or `:running` label blocks, configured role or not), and each review is proven by the newest close-out record of that role on this PR (outcome done, head not moved), not by the label, which a person with triage access can add, and stands at a head that still covers the current one. The head rule is shared with the reviewer's mark-ready (`internal/prgate`): a head is covered when no path outside `.ard/<issue#>/` changed since the reviewed commit, or, for the merger, when every such path came from `main` (an update-branch merge does not invalidate a review, but a path both the PR and `main` changed since the review does). A stale review is only logged: a person must re-request it; fix rounds rerun reviewers only after a worker fix\ndocs/proposal.md:408:stale: { inactive_for: 30d, suppress_for: 90d }\ndocs/proposal.md:452:- **Kill switch.** Four sources stop new claims, scanner runs (filing and autoclose), stale closes and merges: a local pause file `~/.ard/pause/<owner>/<repo>` outside the repository (the path is lowercased; `ard serve` creates the directory, so `touch` pauses and `rm` resumes), `paused: true` in the `config.yaml` in the host's working tree, `ard:pause` on the pinned status issue, and `paused: true` in the config on `origin/main`. They are checked cheapest first, before every claiming tick. The delay is at most one loop interval plus 10% jitter; a tick already running may finish its claims. `SIGUSR1` (Unix only) makes every loop run one tick now, logged as `controller.wake`; the tick still passes the kill switch and the job pool, and wakes that arrive while a tick runs coalesce into one extra tick. The gate answers one controller-wide question and has no per-kind checks, so every loop that writes (scanner filing and autoclose, stale closes, merges, claims), except the Reaper, the cost-table writer and in-flight close-outs, must report `Claims() == true` and is skipped while paused. Running jobs, in-flight close-outs, the Reaper and the `costs` loop continue: the table is bookkeeping about work already done, not new work. The check fails closed: an unreadable status issue, a failed fetch of `origin/main`, or a config that fails to load counts as paused. The pause file pauses when anything exists at the path (a file, a directory or a symlink; content is ignored), and a path that cannot be examined, for example because `pause/<owner>` is a regular file, also counts as paused. The local working-tree `config.yaml` is read only for its `paused` key; a missing, unreadable or invalid file does not pause by itself, but a readable one that says `paused: true` does. The pause file and the local `paused` are the kill switches that nothing pushed to GitHub can lift. Config and role directories otherwise come from `origin/main` at the same SHA the tick's jobs use (see Configuration). `ard serve` remembers the status issue number in `_controller.db` (`meta` key `status_issue`). At startup it reads that issue directly and adopts it if it is still open, titled `ard status`, carries the `<!-- ard:status -->` marker and has the App as author; otherwise it searches by those same tests, and creates and pins the issue if none matches. Search is eventually consistent, so the remembered number is what stops a quick restart from creating a second issue; if two still match, the lowest number wins. A cached issue that cannot be read for a reason other than not-found falls back to search rather than aborting startup. A dry-run never stores its synthetic (negative) number; a real number found by search is stored only in the separate dry-run database. A second `ard serve` on the same repo exits with an error: the leader lock is an `flock` on `~/.ard/locks/<owner>/<repo>.lock` (local filesystem only), shared by live and dry-run.\ndocs/proposal.md:491:    P0[\"Phase 0: Skeleton<br/>controller and config<br/>label bootstrap<br/>committer, local executor<br/>cve and stale scanners<br/>dry-run mode\"] -->|\"Gate 1: 1 week dry-run, 0 duplicate issues\"| P1[\"Phase 1: First loop<br/>lead + engineer triage<br/>engineer worker<br/>eng-review<br/>Reaper<br/>human approval gate\"]\ndocs/proposal.md:502:Phase 0 runs in dry-run: the controller logs every issue, label and commit it would make, and does none of them. Dry-run keeps its own state (`~/.ard/state-dryrun/`) and records would-file findings under synthetic negative issue numbers, so repeated scans don't re-propose them and `max_open` is exercised. The ledger is `effects.jsonl` in that directory: every intended effect (issue, label, comment, commit, push, merge) is appended as one JSON line, and Gate 1 counts duplicate issues there by repeated `<!-- ard:fp=... -->` markers in `CreateIssue` bodies. Gate 1 needs only the deterministic path (CLI, config, GitHub client, effects, state, labels, executor, committer, controller, pool and the cve and stale scanners); the agent adapter, close-out and redaction are built during the soak week.\ninternal/merger/doc.go:25://     does not invalidate a review (prgate.Covers with the merge base). A stale","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Qj4LCesDgB4y3bHQp9UhHn","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
