\n//     with the reviewer's mark-ready. Each requested review must be done at a\n//     head that still covers the current one. An update-branch merge from main\n//     does not invalidate a review (prgate.Covers with the merge base). A stale\n//     review is only logged: until review fix rounds (#41) a human must\n//     re-request it.\n//  2. The body's first line is \"Closes #<issue>\" and a worker job of the issue\n//     ran on this branch (its id becomes the Ard-Job trailer). Otherwise the PR\n//     gets ard:needs-human.\n//  3. Conflict: only a computed mergeable=false or mergeable_state dirty is a\n//     conflict (ard:needs-human, until #41). A null or unknown mergeable, and\n//     a 405 \"not mergeable\" from the merge call, are GitHub still computing and\n//     mean retry next tick: never a label.\n//  4. Up to date: the branch is behind when main changed outside\n\nOnly ready (non-draft) PRs the App opened on `worker/<role>-<issue#>-<slug>` with base `main` are candidates. The reviewer loop marks a PR ready, but the merger re-checks everything itself and never trusts the draft flag, because a person can mark a PR ready by hand. A PR merges when all of these hold:\n\n- every requested `ard:review:<role>` is `:done` and none is `:changes` or `:running` (any `ard:review:*:changes` or `:running` label blocks, configured role or not), and each review is proven by the newest close-out record of that role on this PR (outcome done, head not moved), not by the label, which a person with triage access can add, and stands at a head that still covers the current one. The head rule is shared with the reviewer's mark-ready (`internal/prgate`): a head is covered when no path outside `.ard/<issue#>/` changed since the reviewed commit, or, for the merger, when every such \n- it carries none of `ard:hold`, `ard:needs-human`, `ard:tests:failed` or `ard:executor:failed`, its issue is open and carries neither `ard:hold` nor `ard:needs-human`, and no worker or review job of the issue is running\n- the first line of the PR body is exactly `Closes #<issue>` (what the worker writes; it is what closes the issue) and the state records a worker job of the issue on this branch (its id is the `Ard-Job` trailer). Otherwise the PR gets `ard:needs-human`\n- GitHub has computed that the branch does not conflict with `main`. Only a computed `mergeable: false` or `mergeable_state: dirty` is a conflict: it gets `ard:needs-human` and one comment, and a follow-up to #41 will turn this into a rebase round (`reviewfix.RequestRound`) that counts toward `max_review_rounds`. A `mergeable` that is still null or unknown, and a 405 \"not mergeable\" from the merge call, mean GitHub is still computing: the merger retries next tick and never labels\n- the branch is up to date with `main`; if it is behind, the merger updates it and checks again next tick on the new head. \"Behind\" means `main` changed something **outside `.ard/<digits>/`** since the merge base: the committer pushes `.ard/<issue#>/` to `main` on every close-out, and counting those would keep every branch behind forever. A history with several merge bases (criss-cross merges) is skipped with a warning, because the choice would decide what counts as the PR's own change. GitHub's merge API guards the head SHA, not the base, so a push to `main` between the merger's fetch and the\n- checks are green on the current head, read from check runs and commit statuses (the Statuses API, which Jenkins and other external CI report through; the App needs Commit statuses: read). A required context is satisfied by either a check run or a commit status of the same name; if both exist the newer wins, and red from either never counts as green. As on GitHub, a rule pinned to an `integration_id` is satisfied only by a status created by that App: the merger resolves the creator's `<slug>[bot]` login to an App id (`GET /apps/{slug}`, cached per process); a status from a user or another App\n  - when the rules are readable and name required checks, each must be completed with `success`, `neutral` or `skipped` on the head (the latest run per name; a rerun in flight hides the old result), or have a latest commit status `success`. Missing or running checks wait; a red one (failure, cancelled, timed out, action required) gets `ard:needs-human` and one comment naming the checks, until #41 adds fix rounds\n  - when the rules are unavailable (a free-plan private repository answers 403 \"upgrade\", or 404) or no required checks apply, there is no list to go by: if the head has no check runs and no commit statuses the merger merges on reviews alone, but only after a grace period since it first saw the head (at least one tick and two minutes), so CI that has not queued yet is not missed; if it has either, every latest run and status per name must be green (a pending status waits)\n  - when the rules cannot be read (a 5xx, a network error, a permissions 403), nothing merges that tick. Rule types ard does not evaluate (`workflows`, `code_scanning`, `required_deployments`) are logged at warn\n- the diff touches no protected path; the list comes only from `merge.protected_paths`, with no floor in code (decided, #3), so a human who empties the list removes the gate. `ard init` proposes the defaults `.github/`, `.ard/config.yaml`, the four role trees (`.ard/scanners/`, `.ard/triage/`, `.ard/workers/`, `.ard/reviewers/`), `**/CODEOWNERS`, and the agent instructions a PR could plant to steer later agents: `CLAUDE.md`, `**/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `**/AGENTS.md`, `.claude/` and `.mcp.json` (13 entries; `**/X` already matches a root `X`, the bare entries are kept for clarity). An entry en\n\nA protected path needs a GitHub review in state `APPROVED`, from a user who is not an App and has write, maintain or admin permission, that stands at a commit the current head still covers (the same rule as for reviews). A later `CHANGES_REQUESTED` or `DISMISSED` review by the same person voids it, a plain comment does not. A triage user cannot push or merge on GitHub, so their approval does not authorise a change to `.github/`, config or agent instructions. The label is not the approval: `ard:needs-approval` is informational, the merger adds it and posts one comment per head, and removing it \n\nIt squash-merges with the PR title plus ` (#<pr>)` (cleaned: whitespace and control characters collapsed, skip-CI tokens defused, closing references neutralised, at most 72 characters) and one `Ard-Job: <id>` trailer line per worker job of the branch, guarded by the head SHA. It then deletes the branch, so `.ard/<issue#>/` and its logs land on `main` with the code, and the `Closes #<issue>` line closes the issue. A merge records a `merge` event in the state store.\n\n296-\tif t.updated {\n297-\t\tt.log.Info(\"merge.waiting_update\", \"pr\", pr.Number, \"reason\", \"another branch was updated this tick\")\n298-\t\treturn\n299-\t}\n300-\tt.updated = true\n301-\terr := d.Effects.UpdateBranch(ctx, pr.Number, pr.HeadSHA)\n302-\tswitch {\n303-\tcase err == nil && t.dry:\n304:\t\tt.log.Info(\"merge.would_update\", \"pr\", pr.Number, \"issue\", issue, \"head\", pr.HeadSHA, \"main\", t.mainTip)\n305-\tcase err == nil:\n306-\t\tt.log.Info(\"merge.update_branch\", \"pr\", pr.Number, \"issue\", issue, \"head\", pr.HeadSHA, \"main\", t.mainTip)\n307-\tcase errors.Is(err, github.ErrConflict):\n308-\t\t// A moved head is a 409 too: only a computed conflict on an\n309-\t\t// unchanged head is one.\n41:func (s *syncBuf) Write(p []byte) (int, error) {\n47:func (s *syncBuf) String() string {\n72:func dryRun(h *harness) { h.ledger = filepath.Join(h.t.TempDir(), \"effects.jsonl\") }\n74:func newHarness(t *testing.T, opts ...option) *harness {\n107:func (h *harness) git(args ...string) string {\n114:func (h *harness) commit(branch, path, body string) string {\n139:func (h *harness) updateBranch(pr int, branch string) string {\n174:func (h *harness) newPR(mut ...func(*spec)) pr {\n219:func (h *harness)