never a label.\n//  4. Up to date: the branch is behind when main changed outside\n\nOnly ready (non-draft) PRs the App opened on `worker/<role>-<issue#>-<slug>` with base `main` are candidates. The reviewer loop marks a PR ready, but the merger re-checks everything itself and never trusts the draft flag, because a person can mark a PR ready by hand. A PR merges when all of these hold:\n\n- every requested `ard:review:<role>` is `:done` and none is `:changes` or `:running` (any `ard:review:*:changes` or `:running` label blocks, configured role or not), and each review is proven by the newest close-out record of that role on this PR (outcome done, head not moved), not by the label, which a person with triage access can add, and stands at a head that still covers the current one. The head rule is shared with the reviewer's mark-ready (`internal/prgate`): a head is covered when no path outside `.ard/<issue#>/` changed since the reviewed commit, or, for the merger, when every such \n- it carries none of `ard:hold`, `ard:needs-human`, `ard:tests:failed` or `ard:executor:failed`, its issue is open and carries neither `ard:hold` nor `ard:needs-human`, and no worker or review job of the issue is running\n- the first line of the PR body is exactly `Closes #<issue>` (what the worker writes; it is what closes the issue) and the state records a worker job of the issue on this branch (its id is the `Ard-Job` trailer). Otherwise the PR gets `ard:needs-human`\n- GitHub has computed that the branch does not conflict with `main`. Only a computed `mergeable: false` or `mergeable_state: dirty` is a conflict: it gets `ard:needs-human` and one comment, and a follow-up to #41 will turn this into a rebase round (`reviewfix.RequestRound`) that counts toward `max_review_rounds`. A `mergeable` that is still null or unknown, and a 405 \"not mergeable\" from the merge call, mean GitHub is still computing: the merger retries next tick and never labels\n- the branch is up to date with `main`; if